[=#tdsconfiguration]
= Basic Configuration =

To enable SQL Relay to speak the TDS protocol, add the appropriate listener tag:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@parts/sqlrelay-tdsfebasic.conf@
}}}
}}}

In this configuration:

* The listener tag instructs SQL Relay to load the //tds// protocol module, and listen on port 1433, which is the standard Microsoft SQL Server port.
* The connection tag instructs the instance to log in to the //mssqlodbc// ODBC data source as user //example// with password //example//.

Note the mars=yes parameter in the connect string.  See the [../configreference.html#odbc SQL Relay Configuration Reference] for the //mars// option, which instances using the tds protocol typically need.

The instance can be started using:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
sqlr-start -id example
}}}
}}}

and accessed from the local machine using:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
tsql -H localhost -p 1433 -U example -P example
}}}
}}}

The instance can be stopped using:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
sqlr-stop -id example
}}}
}}}

Since this instance speaks the TDS client-server protocol, any client that wishes to use it must also speak the TDS client-server protocol.  This means that most software written using the !FreeTDS or Microsoft SQL Server APIs, or written using a database abstraction layer which loads a driver for Microsoft SQL Server or SAP/Sybase can access this instance.  However, it also means that SQL Relay's standard sqlrsh client program and client programs for other databases (eg. the mysql, psql, and sqlplus command line programs, !MySQL Workbench, Oracle SQL Developer, etc.) cannot access this instance.

